  "state": {
    "case_id": "97",
    "case_summary": "A 14-year-old girl presented with a 6-year history of a photosensitive eruption involving her face, neck, trunk, and extremities. Her symptoms began at age 8 years. She had previously tried multiple medications including systemic steroids, hydroxychloroquine, methotrexate, mycophenolate mofetil, cyclosporine, tacrolimus, tofacitinib, rituximab, and intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG), with minimal improvement.\n\nClinical examination revealed:- Violaceous erythema on forehead and midface- Diffuse poikiloderma involving upper chest, anterolateral thighs, and extensor arms- Erythematous, flat-topped papules on extensor joints of hands- Nailfold examination showed dilated capillary loops, capillary dropout, and ragged cuticles\n\n- Laboratory tests:  - Serologic workup performed (specific results not provided)  - Elevated serum IFN-β levels (8 pg/mL, normal ≤2 pg/mL)  - Presence of anti-TIF-1γ autoantibodies- Imaging studies:  - Radiographic evidence of myositis (specific findings not detailed)- Pathology:  - Confirmatory skin biopsy performed (specific findings not detailed)",
